A new or extended charging location for heavy electric trucks and buses, open to the public at all times, built and run by the company that applies.
The SPULA (Subsidieregeling Publieke Laadinfrastructuur zwaar vervoer, the Dutch subsidy for public charging infrastructure for heavy transport) is paragraph 2.2 of the Tijdelijke subsidieregeling zero-emissie mobiliteit (a temporary ministerial subsidy scheme for zero-emission mobility). Heavy electric road transport means vehicles of EU categories M3 (buses and coaches), N2 and N3 (goods vehicles above 3.5 tonnes). The charging location must:
- be in the Netherlands and publicly accessible at all times, on public or private land, and open at least 40 hours a week between 09:00 and 21:00;
- have at least 1,400 kW of charging stations of 200 kW or more, including at least two stations of 350 kW or more;
- give every charging point a CCS or MCS connector (Combined Charging System, or Megawatt Charging System for trucks), foundations for vehicles of at least 50 tonnes, a clearance height of at least 4.2 m and space for a 16.5 m tractor-trailer;
- lie on a business park or within 1 km driving distance of a motorway or main road (A- or N-weg), reached by a paved road at least 6 m wide.
Where the location will not have enough grid capacity within two years, the scheme also funds a stationary battery (a battery outside the vehicle that communicates with the charging station). The need is a national policy one: speeding up the roll-out of public charging for heavy electric transport to cut CO2 and air-polluting emissions; other vehicles may also use the location.
